A||||1||007DOUG||Yikes, I caught my Indy Pace Car on fire.||||11||
Z||000000||007DOUG||04-22-2000||01:29 PM||||Everytime I take one of my fieros to a show, I always hear "Duh.....they catch on fire". I drive a fiero everyday, and have Never had fire problems......till last sunday.<P>I bought my fourth Pace Car from John of FieroHeaven. I know alot of posts have been negative about him, but I had no problems, and the car was exactly as he described. The car had been sitting in his sister's backyard in Illinois for 2 years.<P>The car is a total basket case. I can't believe how some owners treat there cars.  See link for pics of its poor condition and fire damage.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierowarehouse/basketcaseindy.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fierowarehouse/basketcaseindy.html</A> <P>Anyways, I was trying to start the car. I kept cranking and cranking. I could smell gas, and assumed it was flooding. So I gave it a few minutes break, and proceeded to crank on it once again. That is until I heard the familiar sound of a gas grill being lit. Yikes, Flames engulfing the rear of my Indy. <P>To make a long story short (you can read the whole story on the webpage) Once the fire was out, I found a few holes in the gas line. Mouse damage?? The majority of the flames were from all the gas on the floor.<P>Thankfully no indy parts were damaged. <P>I guess I can now say "Duh, them there fieros catch fire"<BR>||||reg||11
Z||000001||Sage||04-22-2000||05:21 PM||||Doug;<P>Sorry to hear about your misfortune.  Glad it was no worse than it was!  I can relate to the felling as my sons 86gt pulled up out in front of the house night before last with flames shooting out from around the rear decklid!<P>This is a car he bought several months ago that did not run when he bought it.  The man he bought it from had paid a "mechanic" to put a used tranny in it, and when he got it back, it would not run.  He said he was tired of putting money in it and just wanted out from under.<P>We played with it and figured out that someone had put the dist. in 180 degrees out.  Switched the wires around and she fired right up!  The wiring harnesses were nowhere near where they belonged and there were no bolts in the drivers tranny rear mount.  Whoever worked on it either didn't know or didn't care what he was doing.  I told my son it needed to be taken apart and put back together correctly, but he just wanted to drive it!<P>I fixed the exhaust leaks, put on new egr tube and drivers rear caliper (which had been left off with the line plugged by a nail!) and told him to stay close to home with it and see what happens.  Well, fire is what happened!  Haven't looked at it real close yet, but from what I could tell at midnight with a flashlight, it looks like the harness under the vacuum canister on the drivers side rear inner fender, shorted out and started the insulation burning, and from there it just spread.  If it ever quits raining, I will determine if it is worth fixing and what it will take to do so.  I have a complete parts car with good harnesses and all necessary parts,(thats where the caliper and brake line came from).  Just wanted you to know you aren't alone!  Have a good one! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||4
